Z||000031||FieroMaster88||02-19-2001||12:50 AM||||I dont know what to say.  I have always watched NASCAR as long as I can remember and dale was my favorite.  He is one of the reasons I want to get into racing.  I want to be known of as one of the greats. I just cant believe it.  This is a sad day for nascar.  My prayers are with his family.<BR><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">       <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe 30,000 miles <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net4/smiling2.gif">  <BR>84 Indy Fiero (restoration Project)  <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/3dlil/eek2.gif"> ||||reg||9
Z||000032||Sleeper||02-19-2001||01:22 AM||||it's kinda trippy how the 21 car pile-up looked so DISASTROUS and you'd think one of the drivers in that would have got killed but a little hit to the side and a smash into the wall and there goes one of the best drivers ever to race period. <P>to me Dale Earnhardt was just a name cuz i mostly watch F1, cart & lemans and i was never really a fan of nascar. but today after watching ESPN and hearing some of Dale's racing acomplishments i was stunned, its just goes to show life is not always fair and your life can be taken from you in a split second even in a simple accident that looks routine. it sucks.<P>so even though i'm not a fan of nascar i still love racing in general and--just like what fieromaster says above: Racers like Dale Earnhardt are people we race fans look up to and make younger adults and kids wake up one morning and say "I wanna do that someday".<P> <P><p>[This message has been edited by Sleeper (edited 02-19-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000033||2M4 Dale||02-19-2001||08:23 AM||||I have to admit that I shed a tear at the news of Dale's passing. Although Dale was never one of my favorite drivers he was the one that I loved to hate. Without Dale's promotion of NASCAR there is no way that any kind of racing would be getting the TV coverage it gets today. Dale Earnhart has done so much to enhance NASCAR and auto racing in general that we'll never be able to replace him. I am astounded at the fact that he died as a result of a selfless attempt to let his son and his newest driver (M. Waltrip) battle it out for the victory. I believe that Dale would be alive this morning (and victor at Daytona) if he had just ran his own race. Instead, he wanted to give his two young drivers a shot at the glory. For all of his rough edges, Dale, was a true champion on and off the track. My prayers go out to his family and friends and fans...God rest his soul.||||reg||9
Z||000034||ChadMan||02-19-2001||08:29 AM||||I can't believe Dale is gone.  Larger than life Dale Earnhardt?  Done in by nosing into the Turn 4 wall at Daytona - after 7 championships and so many nasty-looking crashes and all those wins?<P>It doesn't seem real.||||reg||9
Z||000035||Raydar||02-19-2001||08:46 AM||||A prayer for his family and friends...||||reg||1
Z||000036||mrfixit58||02-19-2001||09:57 AM||||I've only missed one Daytona 500 race since the early 1980's -- Weatherly Box, section Q, Row 13, Seat 1. Big "E" fan since the yellow and blue Wrangler 3 car. This was to be the last time at this race. It had gotten too big -- too much hassle. Seems appropriate now.<P>Rest in peace, Dale. You made a lasting impression in the NASCAR family. You left a big hole when you left. Rest in peace.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P> ||||reg||9
Z||000037||Deer_Slayer||02-19-2001||10:16 AM||||Long live the Intemidator.<P> "Heroes will always be remembered, but legends never die"||||reg||1
Z||000038||fropuff||02-19-2001||11:37 AM||||You know, it's weird because this was the first actual NASCAR race that I watched.  I had never been into watching cars run 250 times around an oval track, and I don't know why I watched this one.  I agree, there should have been more complications with the "larger" crash.  A few scrapes, a cuncussion or two, etc.  Earnhardt's didn't seem that bad on TV, but they say he was going about 180 MPH.  There's many "what-ifs" to this one.  What if he had not tried to correct his spin? He would have lost, but he wouldn't have hit so hard.  What if he was wearing a full-face helmet?  Would that have really saved him?  There are many more questions that everyone is still trying to answer, but all I know is that it was his time to go, and I am kind of reassured that he went doing something he loved very much.  We will miss you Dale...||||reg||1
Z||000039||Wade87GT||02-19-2001||12:19 PM||||I wasn't a big fan since most of the bullies on the road here in the south always tried to imitate the "Intimidator". Not his fault. But Dale's performance at the 24 Hours of Daytona completely changed my attitude about him. He was the "Gentleman Racer" you always read about from road racings past and he showed the international racing community that he can race anywhere. I was really looking forward to seeing him racing at Lemans next year...||||reg||1
Z||000040||deceler8||02-19-2001||03:04 PM||||Absolutely stunned. Didn't hear of his passing until the late news. I saw the entire race and the accident that claimed Mr. Earnhardt didn't seem that bad. Maybe that's why I was so shocked upon hearing the news.<P>In just a split second, the race went from one of the greatest victories for a racing team (with Michael, Dale Jr. and Dale Sr. 1-2-3) to one of the greatest tragedies. And a selfless one at that...you just have to wonder if Dale gave his life so that his fellow driver could finally win one.<P>And you have to think that there is something wrong with the basic design of the cars if one can be killed in a relatively minor accident while walking away from one that basically destroys the car. Maybe the time has come for airbags in Winston Cup cars.||||reg||1
Z||000041||JohnnyK||02-19-2001||05:04 PM||||I don't think an airbag would help your insides from going 185mph-0mph in 1 foot.||||reg||1
Z||000042||DaRkLoRD||02-19-2001||05:31 PM||||Nope.. that much kinetic energy takes a lot to dissipate. but a foam crash system like in Demolition Man would be cool, and since they're already wearing helmets etc. it shouldn't get into their mouths or eyes.<P>Of course, such a system would take years to develop probably...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||9
Z||000043||FieroJoe||02-19-2001||06:25 PM||||My dad has speculated on what happend to dale sr, why such a seemingly light impact had taken his life.  As, Darklord,said, there is an incredible amount of momentum with a 3400 pound car at 180 mph.  With the time of impact so incredible small, all that force is applied in that short period, making the force on the car and Dale himself, very great.  From the reports I have read, it seems as if he suffered a basal skull fracture.  When he hit, his neck whipped around so much that his brain actually turned to mush, and his medulla and cerebellum were disconnected from his spinal cord.  The medulla controls involuntary actions (digestion, respiration, and heartbeat).  With that not controlling his heart and breathing anymore, he was dead almost instantly.  That is why the paramedics could not administer CPR, because there were no connections back to the brain to make his heart and breathing go again.<BR>My dad, a paramedic/firefighter for many years, has seen this exact thing in the field.  Not a pretty sight at all, maybe if Dale had been wearing the head restraint strap this could have been prevented, or maybe not.  All we can do is remember the great Intimidator of the racing world, and send his family and friends our prayers.||||reg||1
Z||000044||theogre||02-19-2001||06:47 PM||||On the head restraint... One item I heard is that only 7 drivers wore the HANS brace yesterday.<P>Would it have helped Dale Sr? No way to know. At one point it looked like the hood penetrated the interior of the car but even if it didn't the 1 2 punch hitting with Shrader and the wall almost at the same time was more than enough.<P>Maybe now NASCAR and the track owners will take a more serious look at using a softer/better wall system. Some tacks are probably due for a major rework of the radius of the turns as well.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000045||ChadMan||02-20-2001||10:39 AM||||I read on Speedvision.com today that a few idiots have sent death threats to Sterling Marlin because of his unfortunate role in Dale's fatal crash.  Unbelievable.||||reg||8
Z||000046||KnightRyder31||02-20-2001||10:55 AM||||Man...thats not cool.<BR>It wasn't Marlin's fault, it just happend.<P>||||reg||1
